  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Friday)\nIssued at 142 PM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nCurrent Water Vapor and upper air models show west-northwest flow\naloft, as upper ridge has built over the western CONUS, with the \nupper high located over the four corners region. 19Z surface \nanalysis shows a frontal boundary running north/south through \neastern Wyoming. Satellite shows clear skies across the forecast \narea. Temperatures sit in the upper 60s and 70s, with breezy winds\nout of the south.\n\nLittle change to the overall pattern is expected over the next \nweek, with zonal to northwest flow aloft hanging around through\nthe period. Mild Upper ridge remains mostly stagnant through the\nweekend, building a bit early next week. This will prompt warming\ntemperatures Monday and Tuesday, with highs reaching into the 80s\nfor some areas. Next disturbance comes Saturday as a wave rides \nthe northwest flow through the Dakotas. Current models keep the\nbulk of better forcing in North Dakota, but some light\nprecipitation could reach down into northwestern SD. Limited \nmoisture in the region, so any precipitation will likely only \namount to a trace or a few hundredths.\n\nUpper ridge continues to dominate the western CONUS through much\nof next week, resulting in temperatures staying above normal, with\na few small chances for light precipitation as weak shortwaves \nride the northwest flow into the northern plains.",
